# The Chloroplast Function Database: a large-scale collection of Arabidopsis

## TECHNICAL ADVANCE
The Chloroplast Function Database: a large-scale collection of Arabidopsis Ds/Spm- or T-DNA-tagged homozygous lines for nuclear-encoded chloroplast proteins, and their systematic phenotype analysis
## SUMMARY
A majority of the proteins of the chloroplast are encoded by the nuclear genome, and are post-translationally targeted to the chloroplast. From databases of tagged insertion lines at international seed stock centers and our own stock, we selected 3246 Ds/Spm (dissociator/suppressor–mutator) transposon- or T-DNA-tagged Arabidopsis lines for genes encoding 1369 chloroplast proteins (about $66\%$ of the 2090 predicted chloroplast proteins) in which insertions disrupt the protein-coding regions. We systematically observed 3-week-old seedlings grown on agar plates, identified mutants with abnormal phenotypes and collected homozygous lines with wild-type phenotypes. We also identified insertion lines for which no homozygous plants were obtained. To date, we have identified 111 lines with reproducible seedling phenotypes, 122 lines for which we could not obtain homozygotes and 1290 homozygous lines without a visible phenotype. The Chloroplast Function Database presents the molecular and phenotypic information obtained from this resource. The database provides tools for searching for mutant lines using Arabidopsis Genome Initiative (AGI) locus numbers, tagged line numbers and phenotypes, and provides rapid access to detailed information on the tagged line resources. Moreover, our collection of insertion homozygotes provides a powerful tool to accelerate the functional analysis of nuclear-encoded chloroplast proteins in Arabidopsis. The Chloroplast Function Database is freely available at http://rarge.psc.riken.jp/chloroplast/. The homozygous lines generated in this project are also available from the various Arabidopsis stock centers. We have donated the insertion homozygotes to their originating seed stock centers.
Keywords: Arabidopsis thaliana, homozygotes, phenotype, plastids, tagged lines.
## INTRODUCTION
Chloroplasts are key components of plant cells because they are responsible for capturing the energy that is subsequently used for all plant metabolic processes. Chloroplasts are believed to be descended from an originally free-living cyanobacterium, and have retained their own genome as a result of this ancestry. However, most genes of cyanobacterial origin have been transferred to the nucleus of the host cell during evolution (Martin et al., 2002). Most chloroplast proteins are encoded by the nuclear genome, synthesized in the cytosol, and post-translationally transported into the chloroplasts. With the exception of proteins that are localized in the outer chloroplast envelope, most of the proteins that function primarily in chloroplasts contain specific cleavable N-terminal presequences, which are referred to as chloroplast transit peptides (cTPs; Bruce, 2000). Richly and Leister (2004) identified 2090 proteins with a targeting signal for chloroplasts, by means of computational prediction of the N-terminal cTP that markedly improved prediction accuracy by using at least three of four predictor approaches (iPSORT, PCLR, Predotar and TargetP). These estimates provide basic data for use in large-scale analysis of nuclearencoded chloroplast-targeted proteins.
Large populations of T-DNA insertion lines generated in several different laboratories are being used intensively for plant functional genomics (Sessions et al., 2002; Alonso et al., 2003; Rosso et al., 2003). To supplement these collections, we have constructed more than 17 000 transposoninsertion lines using Ds transposable elements (Kuromori et al., 2004; Ito et al., 2005). The advantage of transposontagged lines is that most contain a single transposed element, and their unique insertion site is highly correlated with the gene responsible for the mutant phenotype. Moreover, the selectable marker-gene cassette that confers hygromycin resistance is active in Ds-tagged lines (Fedoroff and Smith, 1993). Therefore, a marker gene can be used to determine the exact segregation ratios. Previously, we reported seven Ds-tagged gene knock-out Arabidopsis apg mutants with albino or pale-green phenotypes that had been identified by screening RIKEN Ds insertion lines (Motohashi et al., 2001, 2003, 2007; Noutoshi et al., 2005; Myouga et al., 2006, 2008; Garcia et al., 2008). Results from the phenome analysis of 4000 selected transposon-insertion lines were recently reported, and a searchable database of the morphological phenotypes at various growth stages was made publicly available (Kuromori et al., 2006). About 140 lines that showed clear and reproducible visible phenotypes are now available as phenotypic images in the RAPID database (Kuromori et al., 2006; http://rarge.gsc.riken.go.jp/phenome/). The Ds insertion lines are available from the RIKEN BioResource Center (BRC, http://www.brc.riken.jp/lab/epd/Eng/). Furthermore, the systematic identification of Arabidopsis genes that perform essential functions has recently been reviewed by Meinke et al. (2008). More than 600 mutants with defects in seed development, including many identified through screening of T-DNA insertion lines generated at Syngenta (McElver et al., 2001), are listed in the SeedGenes database (Tzafrir et al., 2003; http://www.seedgenes.org). This large collection of more than 350 genes warrants analysis, and has provided valuable information on essential genes required for seed development.
Here, we systematically collected Arabidopsis homozygous lines with transposon- or T-DNA-insertions for use in the functional analysis of nuclear-encoded chloroplast proteins. A similar approach using a large number of homozygous T-DNA lines is now under way in Michigan State University’s Chloroplast 2010 project (Lu et al., 2008). Our collection of transposon or T-DNA insertion homozygotes, as well as the T-DNA insertion homozygote collection of Michigan State University, are valuable resources for the community of plastid researchers. By visually assessing the mutant phenotype, we identified many mutants with abnormal seedling phenotypes, and mutants with the absence of homozygotes. We have carried out phenotypic characterization of these mutants and the predicted functions of the encoded proteins to discuss their essential roles in chloroplast development during embryogenesis and germination. To enhance the utility of our resource data we have constructed a searchable database, named the Chloroplast Function Database, that provides a means for using the data on this mutant resource and the mutant phenotypes. The homozygous insertion lines are available through the Arabidopsis Biological Resource Center (ABRC) and RIKEN BRC.
## RESULTS
Establishment of a high-throughput method for the collection of Arabidopsis $\pmb{D s}/\pmb{S p m-}$ and T-DNA insertion homozygotes for genes that encode predicted chloroplast proteins
On the basis of computational prediction of cTPs, Richly and Leister (2004) predicted that the Arabidopsis genome encodes 2090 chloroplast proteins. From the RIKEN database of Ds insertion sites (Kuromori et al., 2004; Ito et al., 2005; http://rarge.gsc.riken.go.jp/dsmutant/index.pl), and the Salk Institute SIGnAL T-DNA Express database (Alonso et al., 2003; http://signal.salk.edu), we selected tagged lines with a disrupted gene encoding a predicted chloroplast protein, as a result of insertion of a transposon or T-DNA in the proteincoding region. On 6 January 2005, we extracted 4273 transposon- or T-DNA-tagged lines from the RIKEN, John Innes Centre, University of Wisconsin, Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory and Salk Institute data, corresponding to 1401 nuclear-encoded chloroplast proteins (Table 1). However, to avoid the collection of a large number of lines with multiple alleles of one gene, we chose a maximum of four alleles, even when additional alleles were available. In total, we collected 3246 tagged lines corresponding to 1369 proteins, which account for about $66\%$ of the 2090 predicted chloroplast proteins (Figure 1; Table S1). We assembled phenotype data for 1722 tagged lines in February 2009 (Table 1), when we finished the phenotypic analysis for most of the collection except for some of the SALK lines. Our proposed strategy for the collection of homozygotes is depicted in Figure 2.
The selectable marker-gene cassette that encodes antibiotic- and herbicide-resistance proteins can be used for the genotyping of Ds/Spm transposon-tagged lines. For screening of the insertion homozygotes of 1107 transposon-tagged lines from the RIKEN, Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory and John Innes Centre resources, we examined the germination rate of each of the samples of 60 seeds from 17 lines during three generations on selection medium. The segregation ratios from a self-fertilized heterozygote with Mendelian inheritance should be 3:1 for the resistant and sensitive phenotypes, respectively, whereas a self-fertilized homozygous plant should produce only resistant progeny. If segregation and reassortment of the reporter genes are consistent with Mendelian inheritance, insertion homozygotes should be obtained from all transposon-tagged lines $(\chi^{2}=8.5;$ $\bar{P}<0.01$ ).
Among the T-DNA-tagged lines, the selectable markergene cassette also encodes antibiotic- and herbicide-resistance proteins. However, some of the lines show silencing of these genes after several generations. Thus, it is not unusual for a tagged line to not express the drug resistance phenotype, even if it has the insertion. Therefore, we carried out genotyping of these individuals using PCR analysis (Figure 2). At least eight randomly selected seedlings from two generations were genotyped using gene-specific primer pairs and T-DNA left-border primers (Table S2), and we used the results to determine whether homozygous lines existed. To date, we have genotyped 615 T-DNA-tagged lines from the University of Wisconsin and Salk Institute collections (Table 1).
## Monitoring of phenotypes and collection of insertion homozygotes on germination medium plates
On the basis of our segregation analysis of the 702 Ds-tagged RIKEN lines, we collected 59 mutants $(8.4\%)$ with seedling phenotypes and 62 lines $(8.8\%)$ for which we could not obtain insertion homozygotes. The proportion of mutations with visible phenotypes was higher than the proportion $(3.5\%$ of total plants) in the phenotypic data for Ds-tagged lines reported by Kuromori et al. (2006), probably because of the importance of chloroplast biogenesis for normal plant development (Myouga et al., 2006). Loss of the
