## Collection of $\pmb{D s}/\pmb{S p m-}$ or T-DNA-tagged mutants with seedling phenotypes
We classified mutants showing seedling phenotypes with different colors and aerial shapes into four main phenotypic categories (as well as two lesser categories): albino, pale green, variegated and morphologically abnormal (Figure 3; Table 3). However, such phenotypic classification is difficult because of the lack of clear phenotypic definitions: there were many phenotypic variations between individual mutants within a category. The albino mutants (Figure 3b) exhibited a typical defective-chloroplast phenotype as a result of improper chloroplast development, and normally died shortly after planting in soil. We collected 32 albino mutants representing 21 different genes. The pale-green mutants (Figure 3c), which appeared to contain lower levels of chlorophyll and light-harvesting-complex proteins than the wild-type plants (Myouga et al., 2006), required sucrose during the germination stages, but most of them could flower and set seeds when grown in soil. We collected 35 pale-green mutants representing 24 genes. Although the pigment deficiency in mutants with albino or pale-green phenotypes was caused by plastid dysfunction, the plastids of the albino mutants appeared to be much more severely impaired than those of the pale-green mutants (Myouga et al., 2008). On the other hand, the mutants with variegated leaves (Figure 3d) showed patches of green and yellow regions of the leaves, and have been poorly understood at the molecular level in Arabidopsis (for a review, see Sakamoto, 2003). We collected seven lines of mutants with variegated leaves representing five genes, including two previously known for variegated leaves: Thylakoid formation 1 (Thf1; Wang et al., 2004) and Chloroplast Import Apparatus 1 (CIA1; Hung et al., 2004). Several lines exhibited morphologically abnormal seedlings with small or narrow leaves, a dwarf phenotype or a combination of these traits (Figure 3e). We collected 28 mutant lines with visible morphological abnormalities, which defined 21 genes. Many morphological mutants were identified in our collection, also supporting the importance of normal chloroplasts in plant growth and development.
## Collection of multiple mutant alleles
Previously, the large-scale collection of Arabidopsis mutants with abnormal phenotypes was achieved by means of mutagenesis experiments with ethyl-methane sulfonate (EMS) treatment (Guti ´errez-Nava et al., 2004), and by the development of T-DNA tagging lines (McElver et al., 2001),
Ds transposon tagging lines (Kuromori et al., 2006) and activation-tagging lines (Ichikawa et al., 2003; Nakazawa et al., 2003). However, it is possible that the insertion site does not correspond to genes that cause visible phenotypes if the insertion lines have additional insertions, or have another mutation close to the insertion site. To confirm the linkage between mutated genes and visible phenotypes, the reproducibility of the growth phenotypes of other tagged mutant alleles is important. The SIGnAL T-DNA Express database search in January 2008 led to the identification of additional mutant alleles, because the sequence-indexed library of tagged lines that was available in 2008 contained much more data than in 2005. We found that more than half of the genes in the mutant collections in which tagged knock-out mutants showed seedling phenotypes (43 of 76 genes) had duplicated mutant alleles. To investigate whether these mutant alleles at different positions showed the same phenotype, we therefore collected the duplicated mutant alleles from different T-DNA-tagged line collections: 18 Salk Institute T-DNA lines (Alonso et al., 2003), two GABIkat T-DNA lines (Rosso et al., 2003) and four FLAG T-DNA lines (Samson et al., 2002). These duplicated mutant alleles showed phenotypes similar to those of the original tagged alleles (Table 4). These results suggest that transposon or T-DNA-tagged lines with multiple mutant alleles can be used to confirm the genes responsible for mutant phenotypes. We found that eight genes of multiple tagged mutant alleles showed both seedling phenotypes and no homozygotes (Table 4). Disruption of the nuclear genes involved in plastid development may have caused abnormal embryogenesis, as well as the various abnormal phenotypes.
## Characterization of mutants for which no insertion homozygotes were obtained
Mutants for which we could not obtain a knock-out insertion homozygote probably arose from the developmental arrest of homozygous seeds in the silique. Using microscopy, we examined whether an increased proportion of abnormal seeds was apparent in the siliques of selfed heterozygotes during seed development. In 62 of the RIKEN Ds-tagged lines for which we could not obtain homozygotes, the proportion of abnormal seeds in the developing silique ranged from $3.8\%$ to $42.4\%$ (with a mean of $23.7\%$ , which was a much higher proportion than that $(2.9\%)$ of the Nossen wild-type ecotype (Figure 4). The failure to translate some plastid genes causes embryo-lethality in Arabidopsis (Schmitz-Linneweber and Small, 2008), which is consistent with our findings that nine of 87 mutant genes $(10\%)$ function in translation (seven encode a subunit of the ribosomal proteins, and two genes encode a t-RNA synthetase and ligase). These results suggest that genetic disruption by means of Ds insertion in 62 of the RIKEN mutant lines resulted in severe plastid defects and an embryo-lethal phenotype.
Analysis of the predicted functions of chloroplast proteins based on visible mutant phenotypes
We analyzed the predicted functions of chloroplast proteins for which transposon or T-DNA insertions caused phenotypic changes, using the gene ontology (GO) categories for these genes, available through The Arabidopsis Information Resource (TAIR), to see which categories were associated with the different phenotypic classes (Berardini et al., 2004). However, we found little difference in the proportions of the GO molecular functions between genes that resulted in no visible phenotype and the mutant genes that resulted in both abnormal seedling phenotypes and the absence of homozygotes (Figure S1). The GO molecular functions of the listed genes were annotated largely as: unknown $(13-18\%)$ , other enzyme activity $(15-19\%)$ or other binding $(7-12\%)$ . In contrast, the proportions of nucleotide binding, kinase, transcription factor and structural molecular activity functions differed among the three phenotypic classes. Compared with genes for which the insertion homozygotes produce no visible phenotypes, the mutant genes with abnormal seedling phenotypes showed increased proportions in the category of kinase activity, and reduced proportions in the category of transcription factor activity. Among the mutant genes with no homozygotes, we observed increased proportions in both nucleotide binding and structural molecule activity, and reduced proportions of kinase activity. However, we could not clearly identify the molecular mechanisms represented in the different phenotypic classes based on the GO molecular functions. This may be because the broad GO categories may not reflect biological reality, because the categories resulted from computationally derived annotations. These results also reflect the complexity of molecular mechanisms that regulate mutant phenotypes of both abnormal seedling phenotypes and lines with no homozygotes.
Next, we investigated whether the mutant genes described here have been previously described. Of the 76 genes responsible for abnormal seedling phenotypes, 34 $(45\%)$ were not reported previously, and of the 91 genes responsible for a lack of homozygotes $(64\%)$ , 58 were not reported previously (Figure S2; Table S7). These mutant genes were reported between 2003 and the present day (Figure S2).
Based on our computational analysis using version 18 of the Sanger Pfam database (Finn et al., 2006; http://pfam.
sanger.ac.uk/) and novel protein domains, we characterized genes in an Arabidopsis paralogous family, and then compared the numbers of singletons, doubletons and others between genes that produced a wild-type phenotype and mutant genes that produced both abnormal seedling phenotypes and the absence of homozygotes (Table 5). In the Arabidopsis genome, 15.7 and $51.0\%$ of proteins were classified into singleton and doubleton protein families, respectively. The proportion of singletons increased slightly in the genes encoding a putative nuclear-encoded chloroplast protein, whereas doubletons remained nearly the same. However, the opposite occurred for genes with a wild-type phenotype: the number of singletons remained nearly the same, whereas the number of doubletons increased. We found that singletons in mutant genes (both abnormal seedling phenotype and no homozygote) were twice as abundant as they were in both the Arabidopsis genome and the genes that produced a wild-type phenotype. Doubletons were less abundant in the mutants than in the wild type (Table 5). These results suggest that the mutant genes involved in phenotypic changes are indispensable for the proper function of the chloroplasts, because their function cannot be complemented by paralogous genes.
## Database
We have developed a publicly accessible web-based resource, the Chloroplast Function Database (http:// rarge.psc.riken.jp/chloroplast/), to provide access to all currently available knowledge on the tagged lines for nuclearencoded chloroplast proteins, including their morphological phenotype, without any special registration process being required: visitors to the site only require a standard web browser. The database can be used for the following purposes: to obtain detailed information on our project; to search by the Arabidopsis Genome Initiative (AGI) locus ID, mutant line number, protein function or phenotypic category; to obtain lists of genes and mutants that match the phenotypic category; and to obtain the sequence data of 609 PCR primer sets. When a mutant line number and a phenotypic category are selected in the database, detailed information on the corresponding mutant can be displayed (Figure S3). The detailed information page for each mutant line includes: the AGI locus ID, based on the flanking sequences of the transposon or T-DNA line; photographs of the mutants growing on the plates; the selection medium used for plant growth; the phenotypic category; and the inheritance pattern and segregation data. When the AGI locus ID is used as the search keyword, the results indicate the presence of any additional mutant alleles. A project that focuses on obtaining phenotypic observations of the Salk Institute T-DNA-tagged lines is now in progress, and the mutant phenotype database will be made available. The Chloroplast Function Database is an ongoing project of RIKEN, and will be updated as new data become available. All the seed stocks described in this report are available through the RIKEN BRC, ABRC, the Nottingham Arabidopsis Stock Centre and the INRA-Versailles Genomic Resource Centre. We have also sent copies of our insertion homozygous lines to ABRC and RIKEN BRC.
## DISCUSSION
We have collected and analyzed a large number of Ds transposon-tagged and T-DNA-tagged Arabidopsis mutant lines for nuclear-encoded chloroplast proteins, to support functional analysis based on phenotyping of the mutants. We have chosen 2090 as the predicted number of chloroplast proteins according to the report by Richly and Leister (2004), who used a conservative prediction strategy based on the transit peptide sequence. This number is now thought to be too low, because Jarvis (2008) has predicted about 3000 chloroplast proteins. Moreover, some of the genes in the present study may encode proteins that are not plastid proteins. Some proteins are targeted both to plastids and to other cellular compartments (Millar et al., 2006). The GO cellular component annotations of the 2090 genes from TAIR suggest that these genes are concentrated in the chloroplast and plastids $(46\%)$ , followed by other intracellular components $(16\%)$ , other cytoplasmic components $(14\%)$ and other membranes $(9\%)$ . Other subcellular organelles, except chloroplasts and plastids, including mitochondria, the endoplasmic reticulum and the Golgi apparatus, account for less than $4\%$ of all GO cellular component categories. Thus, 2090 is a conservative estimate of the number of chloroplast-targeted proteins, but despite this, it is a useful starting point for a large-scale analysis of this nature.
Using the tagged line collections, we isolated 233 mutants representing 159 genes ( $19\%$ of the genes) that exhibited either seedling phenotypes or embryo-lethal phenotypes (Table 3), and 64 of the 159 genes had multiple mutant alleles that showed nearly the same phenotypes. The database contains the genes described here and those described in previous reports. There were 71 previously reported genes among the 159 genes recorded (Table S7). Data on their mutant phenotype and annotated functions are also available from TAIR (http://www.arabidopsis.org) or the National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI; http:// www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov) (Table S8). These results suggest that our mutant resource and phenotype data provide highly reliable linkages between the phenotypes and genotypes of the nuclear-encoded chloroplast proteins. The relationships between genotypes and phenotypes are important for a systematic approach to the functional analysis of Arabidopsis genes. Our database provides a useful comprehensive information resource to help researchers connect individual Arabidopsis genes to plastid functions, based on phenotype analysis of our tagged mutant collection. It contains mutant phenotypes such as visible chlorophyll deficiencies, altered morphology and defective embryos, but does not contain information on the molecular functions of the disrupted genes. However, it provides a useful resource to help researchers select mutant lines for further analysis of gene functions.
An inherent limitation of our mutant collection is that it does not include other organisms that could be useful for functional analyses of genes that are difficult to study in Arabidopsis. For example, many mutants in the embryolethal category can be informatively studied (i.e. direct molecular functions can be inferred) in Zea mays (maize), where the mutants produce albino seedlings. This is likely to result from differences in gene content in the plastid genomes of the two species, which make chloroplast translation less important for embryogenesis in maize than in Arabidopsis. A fundamental difference between the splicing of plastid group-II introns in Arabidopsis and maize was noted in the CAF2 (Asakura and Barkan, 2006), PPR4 (Schmitz-Linneweber et al., 2006) and PPR5 (Beick et al., 2008) proteins.
In T-DNA-tagged lines from the University of Wisconsin and the Salk Institute, T-DNA insertions in the genes could not be confirmed at high frequency. These findings were probably caused by the inaccurate mapping of the insertion sites in these collections as a result of bulk sequencing of amplified T-DNA flanking regions, in which multiple products were obtained and their start sites were not correctly sequenced. This caused most of the errors in T-DNA insertion sites that were shifted by several hundred bases from the correct sites. To solve these problems, it is necessary to design new primers that will allow the amplification of much longer than expected PCR products during genotyping of the T-DNA-tagged lines.
Perspectives on phenome analysis of tagged lines for the analysis of chloroplast functions
In this study, we have collected a large number of insertion homozygotes of $D s/S p m\r$ or T-DNA-tagged lines. Our collection of homozygous mutants in the Chloroplast Function Database is a powerful tool for the screening of chloroplast mutants, even when the mutants produce phenotypes with no visible abnormalities. For example, it is possible to identify chloroplast-division mutants that have increased plastid number and extraordinary plastid shape by means of microscopic observation; photosynthetic mutants with altered photosynthetic-fluorescence parameters by means of instrumental measurements; and biotic or abiotic stress response-related mutants by means of biological or biochemical measurements. For example, by screening for crr mutants with altered NAD(P)H dehydrogenase activity using our homozygous RIKEN Ds-tagged lines, Okuda et al. (2007) identified crr21 mutants for the pentatricopeptide repeat proteins involved in chloroplast RNA editing by means of pulse-amplitude-modulated fluorometry. For genes without insertion homozygotes, linkages between the mutated genes and the observed phenotypes have not yet been confirmed. Comprehensive analysis of the seed phenotypes that have been identified is ongoing (D.W. Meinke, personal communication).
We collected tagged lines for genes that encode predicted chloroplast proteins that were identified by computational prediction of the N-terminal cTPs (Richly and Leister, 2004), cleavable via the translocon at the outer/inner envelope membrane of chloroplasts (TOC/TIC) translocation machinery (Soll and Schleiff, 2004). Thus, our tagged line collection does not contain genes for the plastid envelope membrane proteins without predicted transit peptides at their
N-terminal end, which are directly transferred from the cytoplasm into the chloroplast independently of the transit peptide (Hofmann and Theg, 2005). Moreover, a recent study demonstrated the existence of a novel chloroplast-targeting pathway, which has been named the ‘endoplasmic reticulum to Golgi apparatus to chloroplast transport pathway’, which starts with protein entry into the endoplasmic reticulum, and also involves the Golgi apparatus (Villarejo et al., 2005). The functional analysis of mutant genes for chloroplast membrane proteins becomes important for identifying various transporters of metabolites, and some receptors of chloroplast signaling. We are planning to use chloroplast proteome data based on mass spectrometry analysis that is available from the Arabidopsis Subcellular Database (SUBA) (Heazlewood et al., 2007; http://www.plantenergy.uwa.edu. au/applications/suba2/index.php) to identify novel nuclearencoded chloroplast proteins, and to add their mutants to our resource to improve functional analysis of the novel chloroplast proteins.
The present study provides a large-scale collection and phenotype analysis of single gene knock-out lines for nuclear-encoded chloroplast proteins. Most of the genes generally have homologs with complementary gene action, and comprise gene families with several members (Briggs et al., 2006). This genetic redundancy is a problem for functional analysis using gene knock-out mutants. Thus, our collection of single gene knock-out lines may not be sufficient to analyze the function of diverse families of nuclear genes, such as those that encode chloroplast proteins. Future studies should focus on examining the phenotypic consequences of multiple gene knock-out lines by crossing insertion homozygotes for genes in a family.
## EXPERIMENTAL PROCEDURES
## Plant materials and growth conditions
The RIKEN Ds-tagged lines were generated by the RIKEN Arabidopsis saturation mutagenesis project (Ito et al., 2002, 2005; Kuromori et al., 2004). Part of the phenotype data for these lines overlaps with the RIKEN Arabidopsis Phenome Information Database (RAPID; Kuromori et al., 2006). Information on the other collections of tagged lines can be obtained from the T-DNA express Web site (http://signal.salk.edu). Seeds from other tagged lines came from John Innes Centre genetrap lines (Sundaresan et al., 1995) and Spm lines (Tissier et al., 1999), University of Wisconsin T-DNA lines (Sussman et al., 2000), Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory genetrap lines (Martienssen, 1998) and the Salk Institute T-DNA lines (Alonso et al., 2003). To assess the linkage between the mutated gene and the seedling’s visible phenotype, we obtained additional alleles from the Genomanalyse im biologischen System Pflanze (GABI-kat; http://www.gabi-kat.de) T-DNA lines (Rosso et al., 2003) and the Flanking Sequence Tag (FLAG; http://urgv.evry.inra.fr/FLAGdb) T-DNA lines (Samson et al., 2002).
We plated seeds that had been surface-sterilized with chlorine gas on GM agar plates (Motohashi et al., 2003) containing $1\%$ sucrose, with the appropriate selection agent (antibiotic or herbicide) for a specific genotype. Plants were stored at $4^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 3 days to improve germination rates, and were then grown in lit growth chambers (CF-405; TOMY-Seiko, http://bio.tomys.co.jp) with approximately $75\ \mathrm{\textmumol}$ photon $\mathsf{m}^{-2}\thinspace\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ at $22^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ under a 16-h light/ 8-h dark cycle (long-day conditions) for 3 weeks.
## Selection of homozygous transposon lines by segregation analysis
We plated 60 seeds per plant on GM agar plates containing an appropriate antibiotic or herbicide, and then identified the homozygous and heterozygous plants from the germination percentage of the seed progeny. When the seeds obtained from a selfed heterozygote are grown, the germination rates of the 60 seeds that are resistant to or sensitive to an antibiotic or herbicide is 3:1, respectively. By contrast, all seedlings from seeds obtained from a homozygous plant will be resistant to an antibiotic or herbicide. The Ds transposon of the RIKEN tagged lines contains the bacterial aph4 gene, which confers resistance to hygromycin (Ito et al., 2002). The Ds transposon of the Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory lines and the John Innes Centre Gene Trap (GT) lines contains the bacterial nptII gene, which confers kanamycin resistance (Sundaresan et al., 1995). The suppressor–mutator $(S p m)$ transposon of the John Innes Centre Spm (SM) lines contains the bacterial bar gene, which confers bialaphos resistance (Tissier et al., 1999). We defined the seeds of tagged lines that could be obtained from several international seed stock centers as the $\mathsf{F}_{1}$ generation, except for the RIKEN Ds-tagged lines, for which we used seeds from the $\mathsf{F}_{3}$ generation. We obtained the genotypic information of the plants during three generations of the tagged lines (one $\mathsf{F}_{3}$ seed, eight $\mathsf{F}_{4}$ seeds and eight $\mathsf{F}_{5}$ seeds from RIKEN; one $\mathsf{F}_{1}$ seed, eight $\mathsf{F}_{2}$ seeds and eight $\mathsf{F}_{3}$ seeds from Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory and the John Innes Centre), from which the segregation ratios can be calculated. In total, we examined the germination rates of 60 seeds from each of 17 plants per line, and observed their segregation ratios and phenotypes. When it was uncertain whether the mutant phenotype was caused by the selection medium (especially the kanamycin medium, which selected for an albino phenotype), the mutant was plated again on GM medium that did not contain a selection agent, and the phenotype was observed.
## Selection of homozygous T-DNA-tagged lines
We sowed the $\mathsf{F}_{1}$ seeds of the University of Wisconsin T-DNAtagged lines (Sussman et al., 2000) on GM plates containing bialaphos to select for the resistance marker used in the T-DNA insertion, and transferred eight plants from Petri dishes into pots with soil. We then maintained the plants under long-day conditions for 2 months and obtained their progeny $(\mathsf{F}_{2}$ plants A–H). We genotyped more than eight plants of the $\mathsf{F}_{2}$ generation to identify homozygotes by means of polymerase chain reaction (PCR) analysis, as described later in this section. When we could not obtain homozygous plants in the $\mathsf{F}_{2}$ generation, we further examined the lines by genotyping 12 plants from the $\mathsf{F}_{3}$ generation. We sowed the $\mathsf{F}_{1}$ seeds of the Salk Institute T-DNA-tagged lines (Alonso et al., 2003) on GM plates without kanamycin because some of the lines did not show a drugresistant phenotype, on account of the silencing of the marker gene. We directly genotyped at least eight (from lines 1–200) or 20 (from lines 301–600) plants of the $\mathsf{F}_{1}$ generation, and determined whether homozygous lines existed. If we could not obtain homozygous plants in the $\mathsf{F}_{1}$ generation, we then genotyped up to 20 plants of the $\mathsf{F}_{2}$ generation.
For our PCR analysis, we picked a few leaves per plant from plants grown on pots containing soil, and then extracted genomic DNA from the leaf tissue using the Wizard Magnetic 96 DNA Plant System (Promega, http://www.promega.com) on a Biomek NX workstation (Beckman Coulter, http://www.beckmancoulter.com). PCR amplification to confirm the insertion of T-DNA was carried out using GoTaq DNA polymerase (Promega) as follows: initial denaturation for 1 min at $94^{\circ}\mathsf{C};$ 35 cycles of $94^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $30\thinspace\mathsf{s},\thinspace55^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $30\ \mathsf{s},$ and $72^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 1 min; and a final extension step at $72^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 5 min. We used genomic DNA to design primer pairs (Table S2) for amplifying approximately 1-kbp DNA fragments that flanked the predicted T-DNA insertion locus, and allowed detection of the T-DNA insertion in each gene. Reactions were performed with an ABI (http:// www.appliedbiosystems.com) GeneAmp 9600 thermocycler. PCR products were separated in $1.8\mathrm{-}2.0\%$ agarose gels, stained with ethidium bromide and visualized with an Epi-Light FA500 analyzer (Aisin, http://www.aisin.com). When we could not obtain homozygotes by PCR analysis of each plant of the $\mathsf{F}_{2}$ generation, we genotyped each $\mathsf{F}_{3}$ plant. If we could not obtain homozygotes in the $\mathsf{F}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{F}_{3}$ generations, we defined these lines as mutants for which we could not produce homozygotes.
## Image recording and database
After suitable training, our experienced assistants systematically observed and recorded the visible seedling phenotype of each line that grew under constant conditions on the agar plates. Hereafter, we have defined ‘mutant’ phenotypes as plants that differed visibly from the wild-type plants; plants with no visible differences were defined as wild-type phenotypes. Digital images of the mutants were recorded on a FinePix S1 Pro digital camera (Nikon, http:// www.nikon.com). High-resolution digital images of the siliques of mutants that failed to generate knock-out homozygotes were obtained with a Coolpix 4500 digital camera (Nikon) attached to an MZ12.5 stereomicroscope (Leica, http://www.leica.com). All the genomic and phenotypic data have been organized into a relational database, created under the RARGE (RIKEN Arabidopsis Genome Encyclopedia) database management system and hosted on the RIKEN server (http://rarge.gsc.riken.jp/). This site provides access to the data through a form-based web interface. The database can be searched by AGI locus ID, tagged line number and phenotype. The output contains details of each plant that matches the search criteria and images of the mutants.
